      {
        "path": "fs",
        "kind": "module",
        "url": "http://docs.rust-lang.org/nightly/std/fs/index.html",
        "docs": [
          {
            "type": "truncatedBlock",
            "nodes": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "spans": [
                  {
                    "text": "Filesystem manipulation operations.",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "spans": [
                  {
                    "text": "This module contains basic methods to manipulate the contents of the local",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "filesystem. All methods in this module represent cross-platform filesystem",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "operations. Extra platform-specific functionality can be found in the",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "extension traits of ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "std::os::$platform",
                    "style": "InlineCode"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": ".",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "heading",
                "level": "Title",
                "spans": [
                  {
                    "text": "Time of Check to Time of Use (TOCTOU)",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "spans": [
                  {
                    "text": "Many filesystem operations are subject to a race condition known as \"Time of Check to Time of Use\"",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "(TOCTOU). This occurs when a program checks a condition (like file existence or permissions)",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "and then uses the result of that check to make a decision, but the condition may have changed",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "between the check and the use.",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "spans": [
                  {
                    "text": "For example, checking if a file exists and then creating it if it doesn't is vulnerable to",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "TOCTOU - another process could create the file between your check and creation attempt.",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "spans": [
                  {
                    "text": "Another example is with symbolic links: when removing a directory, if another process replaces",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "the directory with a symbolic link between the check and the removal operation, the removal",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "might affect the wrong location. This is why operations like ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "remove_dir_all",
                    "style": "InlineCode",
                    "url": "http://docs.rust-lang.org/nightly/std/fs/fn.remove_dir_all.html"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " need to use",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "atomic operations to prevent such race conditions.",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "spans": [
                  {
                    "text": "To avoid TOCTOU issues:",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "list",
                "items": [
                  {
                    "content": [
                      {
                        "type": "paragraph",
                        "spans": [
                          {
                            "text": "Be aware that metadata operations (like ",
                            "style": "Plain"
                          },
                          {
                            "text": "metadata",
                            "style": "InlineCode",
                            "url": "http://docs.rust-lang.org/nightly/std/fs/fn.metadata.html"
                          },
                          {
                            "text": " or ",
                            "style": "Plain"
                          },
                          {
                            "text": "symlink_metadata",
                            "style": "InlineCode",
                            "url": "http://docs.rust-lang.org/nightly/std/fs/fn.symlink_metadata.html"
                          },
                          {
                            "text": ") may be affected by",
                            "style": "Plain"
                          },
                          {
                            "text": " ",
                            "style": "Plain"
                          },
                          {
                            "text": "changes made by other processes.",
                            "style": "Plain"
                          }
                        ]
                      }
                    ]
                  },
                  {
                    "content": [
                      {
                        "type": "paragraph",
                        "spans": [
                          {
                            "text": "Use atomic operations when possible (like ",
                            "style": "Plain"
                          },
                          {
                            "text": "File::create_new",
                            "style": "InlineCode",
                            "url": "http://docs.rust-lang.org/nightly/std/fs/struct.File.html#method.create_new"
                          },
                          {
                            "text": " instead of checking existence then creating).",
                            "style": "Plain"
                          }
                        ]
                      }
                    ]
                  },
                  {
                    "content": [
                      {
                        "type": "paragraph",
                        "spans": [
                          {
                            "text": "Keep file open for the duration of operations.",
                            "style": "Plain"
                          }
                        ]
                      }
                    ]
                  }
                ]
              }
            ],
            "level": "SingleLine"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "path": "hash",
        "kind": "module",
        "url": "http://docs.rust-lang.org/nightly/std/hash/index.html",
        "docs": [
          {
            "type": "truncatedBlock",
            "nodes": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "spans": [
                  {
                    "text": "Generic hashing support.",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "spans": [
                  {
                    "text": "This module provides a generic way to compute the ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "hash",
                    "style": "Plain",
                    "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hash_function"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " of a value.",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "Hashes are most commonly used with ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "HashMap",
                    "style": "InlineCode",
                    "url": "https://doc.rust-lang.org/nightly/std/index.html?search=std%3A%3A%2E%2F%2E%2E%2Fstd%2Fcollections%2Fstruct%2EHashMap"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " and ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "HashSet",
                    "style": "InlineCode",
                    "url": "https://doc.rust-lang.org/nightly/std/index.html?search=std%3A%3A%2E%2F%2E%2E%2Fstd%2Fcollections%2Fstruct%2EHashSet"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": ".",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "spans": [
                  {
                    "text": "The simplest way to make a type hashable is to use ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "#[derive(Hash)]",
                    "style": "InlineCode"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": ":",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "heading",
                "level": "Title",
                "spans": [
                  {
                    "text": "Examples",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "codeBlock",
                "lang": "rust",
                "code": "use std::hash::{DefaultHasher, Hash, Hasher};\n\n#[derive(Hash)]\nstruct Person {\n    id: u32,\n    name: String,\n    phone: u64,\n}\n\nlet person1 = Person {\n    id: 5,\n    name: \"Janet\".to_string(),\n    phone: 555_666_7777,\n};\nlet person2 = Person {\n    id: 5,\n    name: \"Bob\".to_string(),\n    phone: 555_666_7777,\n};\n\nassert!(calculate_hash(&person1) != calculate_hash(&person2));\n\nfn calculate_hash<T: Hash>(t: &T) -> u64 {\n    let mut s = DefaultHasher::new();\n    t.hash(&mut s);\n    s.finish()\n}"
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "spans": [
                  {
                    "text": "If you need more control over how a value is hashed, you need to implement",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "the ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "Hash",
                    "style": "InlineCode",
                    "url": "https://doc.rust-lang.org/nightly/core/hash/index.html?search=core%3A%3Ahash%3A%3AHash"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " trait:",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "codeBlock",
                "lang": "rust",
                "code": "use std::hash::{DefaultHasher, Hash, Hasher};\n\nstruct Person {\n    id: u32,\n    name: String,\n    phone: u64,\n}\n\nimpl Hash for Person {\n    fn hash<H: Hasher>(&self, state: &mut H) {\n        self.id.hash(state);\n        self.phone.hash(state);\n    }\n}\n\nlet person1 = Person {\n    id: 5,\n    name: \"Janet\".to_string(),\n    phone: 555_666_7777,\n};\nlet person2 = Person {\n    id: 5,\n    name: \"Bob\".to_string(),\n    phone: 555_666_7777,\n};\n\nassert_eq!(calculate_hash(&person1), calculate_hash(&person2));\n\nfn calculate_hash<T: Hash>(t: &T) -> u64 {\n    let mut s = DefaultHasher::new();\n    t.hash(&mut s);\n    s.finish()\n}"
              }
            ],
            "level": "SingleLine"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "path": "io",
        "kind": "module",
        "url": "http://docs.rust-lang.org/nightly/std/io/index.html",
        "docs": [
          {
            "type": "truncatedBlock",
            "nodes": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "spans": [
                  {
                    "text": "Traits, helpers, and type definitions for core I/O functionality.",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "spans": [
                  {
                    "text": "The ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "std::io",
                    "style": "InlineCode"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " module contains a number of common things you'll need",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "when doing input and output. The most core part of this module is",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "the ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "Read",
                    "style": "InlineCode",
                    "url": "http://docs.rust-lang.org/nightly/std/io/trait.Read.html"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " and ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "Write",
                    "style": "InlineCode",
                    "url": "http://docs.rust-lang.org/nightly/std/io/trait.Write.html"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " traits, which provide the",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "most general interface for reading and writing input and output.",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "heading",
                "level": "Section",
                "spans": [
                  {
                    "text": "Read and Write",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "spans": [
                  {
                    "text": "Because they are traits, ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "Read",
                    "style": "InlineCode",
                    "url": "http://docs.rust-lang.org/nightly/std/io/trait.Read.html"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " and ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "Write",
                    "style": "InlineCode",
                    "url": "http://docs.rust-lang.org/nightly/std/io/trait.Write.html"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " are implemented by a number",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "of other types, and you can implement them for your types too. As such,",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "you'll see a few different types of I/O throughout the documentation in",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "this module: ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "File",
                    "style": "InlineCode",
                    "url": "http://docs.rust-lang.org/nightly/std/fs/struct.File.html"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "s, ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "TcpStream",
                    "style": "InlineCode",
                    "url": "http://docs.rust-lang.org/nightly/std/net/tcp/struct.TcpStream.html"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "s, and sometimes even ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "Vec<T>",
                    "style": "InlineCode",
                    "url": "https://doc.rust-lang.org/nightly/alloc/vec/index.html?search=alloc%3A%3Avec%3A%3AVec"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "s. For",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "example, ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "Read",
                    "style": "InlineCode",
                    "url": "http://docs.rust-lang.org/nightly/std/io/trait.Read.html"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " adds a ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "read",
                    "style": "InlineCode",
                    "url": "https://doc.rust-lang.org/nightly/std/"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " method, which we can use on",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "File",
                    "style": "InlineCode",
                    "url": "http://docs.rust-lang.org/nightly/std/fs/struct.File.html"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "s:",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "codeBlock",
                "lang": "rust",
                "code": "use std::io;\nuse std::io::prelude::*;\nuse std::fs::File;\n\nfn main() -> io::Result<()> {\n    let mut f = File::open(\"foo.txt\")?;\n    let mut buffer = [0; 10];\n\n    // read up to 10 bytes\n    let n = f.read(&mut buffer)?;\n\n    println!(\"The bytes: {:?}\", &buffer[..n]);\n    Ok(())\n}"
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "spans": [
                  {
                    "text": "Read",
                    "style": "InlineCode",
                    "url": "http://docs.rust-lang.org/nightly/std/io/trait.Read.html"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " and ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "Write",
                    "style": "InlineCode",
                    "url": "http://docs.rust-lang.org/nightly/std/io/trait.Write.html"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " are so important, implementors of the two traits have a",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "nickname: readers and writers. So you'll sometimes see 'a reader' instead",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "of 'a type that implements the ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "Read",
                    "style": "InlineCode",
                    "url": "http://docs.rust-lang.org/nightly/std/io/trait.Read.html"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " trait'. Much easier!",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "heading",
                "level": "Section",
                "spans": [
                  {
                    "text": "Seek and BufRead",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "spans": [
                  {
                    "text": "Beyond that, there are two important traits that are provided: ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "Seek",
                    "style": "InlineCode",
                    "url": "http://docs.rust-lang.org/nightly/std/io/trait.Seek.html"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "and ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "BufRead",
                    "style": "InlineCode",
                    "url": "http://docs.rust-lang.org/nightly/std/io/trait.BufRead.html"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": ". Both of these build on top of a reader to control",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "how the reading happens. ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "Seek",
                    "style": "InlineCode",
                    "url": "http://docs.rust-lang.org/nightly/std/io/trait.Seek.html"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " lets you control where the next byte is",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "coming from:",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "codeBlock",
                "lang": "rust",
                "code": "use std::io;\nuse std::io::prelude::*;\nuse std::io::SeekFrom;\nuse std::fs::File;\n\nfn main() -> io::Result<()> {\n    let mut f = File::open(\"foo.txt\")?;\n    let mut buffer = [0; 10];\n\n    // skip to the last 10 bytes of the file\n    f.seek(SeekFrom::End(-10))?;\n\n    // read up to 10 bytes\n    let n = f.read(&mut buffer)?;\n\n    println!(\"The bytes: {:?}\", &buffer[..n]);\n    Ok(())\n}"
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "spans": [
                  {
                    "text": "BufRead",
                    "style": "InlineCode",
                    "url": "http://docs.rust-lang.org/nightly/std/io/trait.BufRead.html"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " uses an internal buffer to provide a number of other ways to read, but",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "to show it off, we'll need to talk about buffers in general. Keep reading!",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "heading",
                "level": "Section",
                "spans": [
                  {
                    "text": "BufReader and BufWriter",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "spans": [
                  {
                    "text": "Byte-based interfaces are unwieldy and can be inefficient, as we'd need to be",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "making near-constant calls to the operating system. To help with this,",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "std::io",
                    "style": "InlineCode"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " comes with two structs, ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "BufReader",
                    "style": "InlineCode",
                    "url": "http://docs.rust-lang.org/nightly/std/io/buffered/bufreader/struct.BufReader.html"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " and ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "BufWriter",
                    "style": "InlineCode",
                    "url": "http://docs.rust-lang.org/nightly/std/io/buffered/bufwriter/struct.BufWriter.html"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": ", which wrap",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "readers and writers. The wrapper uses a buffer, reducing the number of",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "calls and providing nicer methods for accessing exactly what you want.",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "spans": [
                  {
                    "text": "For example, ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "BufReader",
                    "style": "InlineCode",
                    "url": "http://docs.rust-lang.org/nightly/std/io/buffered/bufreader/struct.BufReader.html"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " works with the ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "BufRead",
                    "style": "InlineCode",
                    "url": "http://docs.rust-lang.org/nightly/std/io/trait.BufRead.html"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " trait to add extra",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "methods to any reader:",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "codeBlock",
                "lang": "rust",
                "code": "use std::io;\nuse std::io::prelude::*;\nuse std::io::BufReader;\nuse std::fs::File;\n\nfn main() -> io::Result<()> {\n    let f = File::open(\"foo.txt\")?;\n    let mut reader = BufReader::new(f);\n    let mut buffer = String::new();\n\n    // read a line into buffer\n    reader.read_line(&mut buffer)?;\n\n    println!(\"{buffer}\");\n    Ok(())\n}"
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "spans": [
                  {
                    "text": "BufWriter",
                    "style": "InlineCode",
                    "url": "http://docs.rust-lang.org/nightly/std/io/buffered/bufwriter/struct.BufWriter.html"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " doesn't add any new ways of writing; it just buffers every call",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "to ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "write",
                    "style": "InlineCode",
                    "url": "https://doc.rust-lang.org/nightly/std/"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": ":",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "codeBlock",
                "lang": "rust",
                "code": "use std::io;\nuse std::io::prelude::*;\nuse std::io::BufWriter;\nuse std::fs::File;\n\nfn main() -> io::Result<()> {\n    let f = File::create(\"foo.txt\")?;\n    {\n        let mut writer = BufWriter::new(f);\n\n        // write a byte to the buffer\n        writer.write(&[42])?;\n\n    } // the buffer is flushed once writer goes out of scope\n\n    Ok(())\n}"
              },
              {
                "type": "heading",
                "level": "Section",
                "spans": [
                  {
                    "text": "Standard input and output",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "spans": [
                  {
                    "text": "A very common source of input is standard input:",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "codeBlock",
                "lang": "rust",
                "code": "use std::io;\n\nfn main() -> io::Result<()> {\n    let mut input = String::new();\n\n    io::stdin().read_line(&mut input)?;\n\n    println!(\"You typed: {}\", input.trim());\n    Ok(())\n}"
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "spans": [
                  {
                    "text": "Note that you cannot use the ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "?",
                    "style": "InlineCode",
                    "url": "https://doc.rust-lang.org/nightly/std/index.html?search=std%3A%3A%2E%2F%2E%2E%2Fbook%2Fappendix%2D02%2Doperators"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " operator",
                    "style": "Plain",
                    "url": "https://doc.rust-lang.org/nightly/std/index.html?search=std%3A%3A%2E%2F%2E%2E%2Fbook%2Fappendix%2D02%2Doperators"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " in functions that do not return",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "a ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "Result<T, E>",
                    "style": "InlineCode",
                    "url": "https://doc.rust-lang.org/nightly/core/result/index.html?search=core%3A%3Aresult%3A%3AResult"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": ". Instead, you can call ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": ".unwrap()",
                    "style": "InlineCode",
                    "url": "https://doc.rust-lang.org/nightly/std/result/Result/index.html?search=std%3A%3Aresult%3A%3AResult%3A%3Aunwrap"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "or ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "match",
                    "style": "InlineCode"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " on the return value to catch any possible errors:",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "codeBlock",
                "lang": "rust",
                "code": "use std::io;\n\nlet mut input = String::new();\n\nio::stdin().read_line(&mut input).unwrap();"
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "spans": [
                  {
                    "text": "And a very common source of output is standard output:",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "codeBlock",
                "lang": "rust",
                "code": "use std::io;\nuse std::io::prelude::*;\n\nfn main() -> io::Result<()> {\n    io::stdout().write(&[42])?;\n    Ok(())\n}"
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "spans": [
                  {
                    "text": "Of course, using ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "io::stdout",
                    "style": "InlineCode",
                    "url": "http://docs.rust-lang.org/nightly/std/io/stdio/fn.stdout.html"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " directly is less common than something like",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "println!",
                    "style": "InlineCode",
                    "url": "http://docs.rust-lang.org/nightly/std/macro.println.html"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": ".",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "heading",
                "level": "Section",
                "spans": [
                  {
                    "text": "Iterator types",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "spans": [
                  {
                    "text": "A large number of the structures provided by ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "std::io",
                    "style": "InlineCode"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " are for various",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "ways of iterating over I/O. For example, ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "Lines",
                    "style": "InlineCode",
                    "url": "http://docs.rust-lang.org/nightly/std/io/struct.Lines.html"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " is used to split over",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "lines:",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "codeBlock",
                "lang": "rust",
                "code": "use std::io;\nuse std::io::prelude::*;\nuse std::io::BufReader;\nuse std::fs::File;\n\nfn main() -> io::Result<()> {\n    let f = File::open(\"foo.txt\")?;\n    let reader = BufReader::new(f);\n\n    for line in reader.lines() {\n        println!(\"{}\", line?);\n    }\n    Ok(())\n}"
              },
              {
                "type": "heading",
                "level": "Section",
                "spans": [
                  {
                    "text": "Functions",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "spans": [
                  {
                    "text": "There are a number of ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "functions",
                    "style": "Plain",
                    "url": "#functions-1"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " that offer access to various",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "features. For example, we can use three of these functions to copy everything",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "from standard input to standard output:",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "codeBlock",
                "lang": "rust",
                "code": "use std::io;\n\nfn main() -> io::Result<()> {\n    io::copy(&mut io::stdin(), &mut io::stdout())?;\n    Ok(())\n}"
              },
              {
                "type": "heading",
                "level": "Section",
                "spans": [
                  {
                    "text": "io::Result",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "spans": [
                  {
                    "text": "Last, but certainly not least, is ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "io::Result",
                    "style": "InlineCode",
                    "url": "http://docs.rust-lang.org/nightly/std/io/error/type.Result.html"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": ". This type is used",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "as the return type of many ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "std::io",
                    "style": "InlineCode"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " functions that can cause an error, and",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "can be returned from your own functions as well. Many of the examples in this",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "module use the ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "?",
                    "style": "InlineCode",
                    "url": "https://doc.rust-lang.org/nightly/std/index.html?search=std%3A%3A%2E%2F%2E%2E%2Fbook%2Fappendix%2D02%2Doperators"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " operator",
                    "style": "Plain",
                    "url": "https://doc.rust-lang.org/nightly/std/index.html?search=std%3A%3A%2E%2F%2E%2E%2Fbook%2Fappendix%2D02%2Doperators"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": ":",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "codeBlock",
                "lang": "rust",
                "code": "use std::io;\n\nfn read_input() -> io::Result<()> {\n    let mut input = String::new();\n\n    io::stdin().read_line(&mut input)?;\n\n    println!(\"You typed: {}\", input.trim());\n\n    Ok(())\n}"
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "spans": [
                  {
                    "text": "The return type of ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "read_input()",
                    "style": "InlineCode"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": ", ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "io::Result<()>",
                    "style": "InlineCode",
                    "url": "http://docs.rust-lang.org/nightly/std/io/error/type.Result.html"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": ", is a very",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "common type for functions which don't have a 'real' return value, but do want to",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "return errors if they happen. In this case, the only purpose of this function is",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "to read the line and print it, so we use ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "()",
                    "style": "InlineCode"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": ".",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "heading",
                "level": "Section",
                "spans": [
                  {
                    "text": "Platform-specific behavior",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "spans": [
                  {
                    "text": "Many I/O functions throughout the standard library are documented to indicate",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "what various library or syscalls they are delegated to. This is done to help",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "applications both understand what's happening under the hood as well as investigate",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "any possibly unclear semantics. Note, however, that this is informative, not a binding",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "contract. The implementation of many of these functions are subject to change over",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "time and may call fewer or more syscalls/library functions.",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "heading",
                "level": "Section",
                "spans": [
                  {
                    "text": "I/O Safety",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "spans": [
                  {
                    "text": "Rust follows an I/O safety discipline that is comparable to its memory safety discipline. This",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "means that file descriptors can be ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "exclusively owned",
                    "style": "Emphasis"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": ". (Here, \"file descriptor\" is meant to",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "subsume similar concepts that exist across a wide range of operating systems even if they might",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "use a different name, such as \"handle\".) An exclusively owned file descriptor is one that no",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "other code is allowed to access in any way, but the owner is allowed to access and even close",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "it any time. A type that owns its file descriptor should usually close it in its ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "drop",
                    "style": "InlineCode"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "function. Types like ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "File",
                    "style": "InlineCode",
                    "url": "http://docs.rust-lang.org/nightly/std/fs/struct.File.html"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " own their file descriptor. Similarly, file descriptors",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "can be ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "borrowed",
                    "style": "Emphasis"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": ", granting the temporary right to perform operations on this file descriptor.",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "This indicates that the file descriptor will not be closed for the lifetime of the borrow, but",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "it does ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "not",
                    "style": "Emphasis"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " imply any right to close this file descriptor, since it will likely be owned by",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "someone else.",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "spans": [
                  {
                    "text": "The platform-specific parts of the Rust standard library expose types that reflect these",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "concepts, see ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "os::unix",
                    "style": "InlineCode",
                    "url": "https://doc.rust-lang.org/nightly/std/../os/unix/index.html?search=std%3A%3A%2E%2E%3A%3Aos%3A%3Aunix%3A%3Aio"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " and ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "os::windows",
                    "style": "InlineCode",
                    "url": "https://doc.rust-lang.org/nightly/std/../os/windows/index.html?search=std%3A%3A%2E%2E%3A%3Aos%3A%3Awindows%3A%3Aio"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": ".",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "spans": [
                  {
                    "text": "To uphold I/O safety, it is crucial that no code acts on file descriptors it does not own or",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "borrow, and no code closes file descriptors it does not own. In other words, a safe function",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "that takes a regular integer, treats it as a file descriptor, and acts on it, is ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "unsound",
                    "style": "Emphasis"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": ".",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "spans": [
                  {
                    "text": "Not upholding I/O safety and acting on a file descriptor without proof of ownership can lead to",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "misbehavior and even Undefined Behavior in code that relies on ownership of its file",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "descriptors: a closed file descriptor could be re-allocated, so the original owner of that file",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "descriptor is now working on the wrong file. Some code might even rely on fully encapsulating",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "its file descriptors with no operations being performed by any other part of the program.",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "spans": [
                  {
                    "text": "Note that exclusive ownership of a file descriptor does ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "not",
                    "style": "Emphasis"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " imply exclusive ownership of the",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "underlying kernel object that the file descriptor references (also called \"open file description\" on",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "some operating systems). File descriptors basically work like ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "Arc",
                    "style": "InlineCode",
                    "url": "https://doc.rust-lang.org/nightly/alloc/sync/index.html?search=alloc%3A%3Async%3A%3AArc"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": ": when you receive an owned",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "file descriptor, you cannot know whether there are any other file descriptors that reference the",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "same kernel object. However, when you create a new kernel object, you know that you are holding",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "the only reference to it. Just be careful not to lend it to anyone, since they can obtain a",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "clone and then you can no longer know what the reference count is! In that sense, ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "OwnedFd",
                    "style": "InlineCode",
                    "url": "https://doc.rust-lang.org/nightly/std/index.html?search=std%3A%3A%2E%2Fos%2Ffd%2Fstruct%2EOwnedFd"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " is",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "like ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "Arc",
                    "style": "InlineCode"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " and ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "BorrowedFd<'a>",
                    "style": "InlineCode",
                    "url": "https://doc.rust-lang.org/nightly/std/index.html?search=std%3A%3A%2E%2Fos%2Ffd%2Fstruct%2EBorrowedFd"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " is like ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "&'a Arc",
                    "style": "InlineCode"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " (and similar for the Windows types). In",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "particular, given a ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "BorrowedFd<'a>",
                    "style": "InlineCode"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": ", you are not allowed to close the file descriptor -- just",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "like how, given a ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "&'a Arc",
                    "style": "InlineCode"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": ", you are not allowed to decrement the reference count and",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "potentially free the underlying object. There is no equivalent to ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "Box",
                    "style": "InlineCode"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " for file descriptors in",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "the standard library (that would be a type that guarantees that the reference count is ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "1",
                    "style": "InlineCode"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "),",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "however, it would be possible for a crate to define a type with those semantics.",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ],
            "level": "SingleLine"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "path": "net",
