                    "text": "Unpin",
                    "style": "InlineCode",
                    "url": "http://docs.rust-lang.org/nightly/core/marker/trait.Unpin.html"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " because they do not have an address-sensitive state like the",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "ones we discussed above. If they did have such a state, those parts of their interface would be",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "unsound without being expressed through pinning, and they would then need to not",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "implement ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "Unpin",
                    "style": "InlineCode",
                    "url": "http://docs.rust-lang.org/nightly/core/marker/trait.Unpin.html"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": ".",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "spans": [
                  {
                    "text": "The compiler is free to take the conservative stance of marking types as ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "Unpin",
                    "style": "InlineCode",
                    "url": "http://docs.rust-lang.org/nightly/core/marker/trait.Unpin.html"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " so long as",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "all of the types that compose its fields are also ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "Unpin",
                    "style": "InlineCode",
                    "url": "http://docs.rust-lang.org/nightly/core/marker/trait.Unpin.html"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": ". This is because if a type",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "implements ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "Unpin",
                    "style": "InlineCode",
                    "url": "http://docs.rust-lang.org/nightly/core/marker/trait.Unpin.html"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": ", then it is unsound for that type's implementation to rely on",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "pinning-related guarantees for soundness, ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "even",
                    "style": "Emphasis"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " when viewed through a \"pinning\" pointer! It is",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "the responsibility of the implementor of a type that relies upon pinning for soundness to",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "ensure that type is ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "not",
                    "style": "Emphasis"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " marked as ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "Unpin",
                    "style": "InlineCode",
                    "url": "http://docs.rust-lang.org/nightly/core/marker/trait.Unpin.html"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " by adding ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "PhantomPinned",
                    "style": "InlineCode",
                    "url": "http://docs.rust-lang.org/nightly/core/marker/struct.PhantomPinned.html"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " field. This is",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "exactly what we did with our ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "AddrTracker",
                    "style": "InlineCode"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " example above. Without doing this, you ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "must not",
                    "style": "Emphasis"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "rely on pinning-related guarantees to apply to your type!",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "spans": [
                  {
                    "text": "If you really need to pin a value of a foreign or built-in type that implements ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "Unpin",
                    "style": "InlineCode",
                    "url": "http://docs.rust-lang.org/nightly/core/marker/trait.Unpin.html"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": ",",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "you'll need to create your own wrapper type around the ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "Unpin",
                    "style": "InlineCode",
                    "url": "http://docs.rust-lang.org/nightly/core/marker/trait.Unpin.html"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " type you want to pin and then",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "opt-out of ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "Unpin",
                    "style": "InlineCode",
                    "url": "http://docs.rust-lang.org/nightly/core/marker/trait.Unpin.html"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " using ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "PhantomPinned",
                    "style": "InlineCode",
                    "url": "http://docs.rust-lang.org/nightly/core/marker/struct.PhantomPinned.html"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": ".",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "spans": [
                  {
                    "text": "Exposing access to the inner field which you want to remain pinned must then be carefully",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "considered as well! Remember, exposing a method that gives access to a",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "Pin",
                    "style": "Plain",
                    "url": "http://docs.rust-lang.org/nightly/core/pin/struct.Pin.html"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "<",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "&mut",
                    "style": "Plain",
                    "url": "http://docs.rust-lang.org/nightly/core/primitive.reference.html"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " InnerT>",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " where ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "InnerT: ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "Unpin",
                    "style": "Plain",
                    "url": "http://docs.rust-lang.org/nightly/core/marker/trait.Unpin.html"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " would allow safe code to",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "trivially move the inner value out of that pinning pointer, which is precisely what you're",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "seeking to prevent! Exposing a field of a pinned value through a pinning pointer is called",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "\"projecting\" a pin, and the more general case of deciding in which cases a pin should be able",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "to be projected or not is called \"structural pinning.\" We will go into more detail about this",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "below",
                    "style": "Plain",
                    "url": "https://doc.rust-lang.org/nightly/core/index.html?search=core%3A%3Aself"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": ".",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "heading",
                "level": "Title",
                "spans": [
                  {
                    "text": "Examples of address-sensitive types",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "heading",
                "level": "Section",
                "spans": [
                  {
                    "text": "A self-referential struct",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "spans": [
                  {
                    "text": "Self-referential structs are the simplest kind of address-sensitive type.",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "spans": [
                  {
                    "text": "It is often useful for a struct to hold a pointer back into itself, which",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "allows the program to efficiently track subsections of the struct.",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "Below, the ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "slice",
                    "style": "InlineCode"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " field is a pointer into the ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "data",
                    "style": "InlineCode"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " field, which",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "we could imagine being used to track a sliding window of ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "data",
                    "style": "InlineCode"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " in parser",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "code.",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "spans": [
                  {
                    "text": "As mentioned before, this pattern is also used extensively by compiler-generated",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "Future",
                    "style": "InlineCode",
                    "url": "http://docs.rust-lang.org/nightly/core/future/future/trait.Future.html"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "s.",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "codeBlock",
                "lang": "rust",
                "code": "use std::pin::Pin;\nuse std::marker::PhantomPinned;\nuse std::ptr::NonNull;\n\n/// This is a self-referential struct because `self.slice` points into `self.data`.\nstruct Unmovable {\n    /// Backing buffer.\n    data: [u8; 64],\n    /// Points at `self.data` which we know is itself non-null. Raw pointer because we can't do\n    /// this with a normal reference.\n    slice: NonNull<[u8]>,\n    /// Suppress `Unpin` so that this cannot be moved out of a `Pin` once constructed.\n    _pin: PhantomPinned,\n}\n\nimpl Unmovable {\n    /// Creates a new `Unmovable`.\n    ///\n    /// To ensure the data doesn't move we place it on the heap behind a pinning Box.\n    /// Note that the data is pinned, but the `Pin<Box<Self>>` which is pinning it can\n    /// itself still be moved. This is important because it means we can return the pinning\n    /// pointer from the function, which is itself a kind of move!\n    fn new() -> Pin<Box<Self>> {\n        let res = Unmovable {\n            data: [0; 64],\n            // We only create the pointer once the data is in place\n            // otherwise it will have already moved before we even started.\n            slice: NonNull::from(&[]),\n            _pin: PhantomPinned,\n        };\n        // First we put the data in a box, which will be its final resting place\n        let mut boxed = Box::new(res);\n\n        // Then we make the slice field point to the proper part of that boxed data.\n        // From now on we need to make sure we don't move the boxed data.\n        boxed.slice = NonNull::from(&boxed.data);\n\n        // To do that, we pin the data in place by pointing to it with a pinning\n        // (`Pin`-wrapped) pointer.\n        //\n        // `Box::into_pin` makes existing `Box` pin the data in-place without moving it,\n        // so we can safely do this now *after* inserting the slice pointer above, but we have\n        // to take care that we haven't performed any other semantic moves of `res` in between.\n        let pin = Box::into_pin(boxed);\n\n        // Now we can return the pinned (through a pinning Box) data\n        pin\n    }\n}\n\nlet unmovable: Pin<Box<Unmovable>> = Unmovable::new();\n\n// The inner pointee `Unmovable` struct will now never be allowed to move.\n// Meanwhile, we are free to move the pointer around.\nlet mut still_unmoved = unmovable;\nassert_eq!(still_unmoved.slice, NonNull::from(&still_unmoved.data));\n\n// We cannot mutably dereference a `Pin<Ptr>` unless the pointee is `Unpin` or we use unsafe.\n// Since our type doesn't implement `Unpin`, this will fail to compile.\n// let mut new_unmoved = Unmovable::new();\n// std::mem::swap(&mut *still_unmoved, &mut *new_unmoved);"
              },
              {
                "type": "heading",
                "level": "Section",
                "spans": [
                  {
                    "text": "An intrusive, doubly-linked list",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "spans": [
                  {
                    "text": "In an intrusive doubly-linked list, the collection itself does not own the memory in which",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "each of its elements is stored. Instead, each client is free to allocate space for elements it",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "adds to the list in whichever manner it likes, including on the stack! Elements can be stored",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "in a stack frame shorter-lived than the collection, provided they are removed from the list",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "before that frame goes out of scope.",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "spans": [
                  {
                    "text": "To make such an intrusive data structure work, every element stores pointers to its predecessor",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "and successor within its own data, rather than having the list structure itself managing those",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "pointers. It is in this sense that the structure is \"intrusive\": the details of how an",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "element is stored within the larger structure \"intrudes\" on the implementation of the element",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "type itself!",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "spans": [
                  {
                    "text": "The full implementation details of such a data structure are outside the scope of this",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "documentation, but we will discuss how ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
