              {
                "type": "heading",
                "level": "Title",
                "spans": [
                  {
                    "text": "Cloning references",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "spans": [
                  {
                    "text": "Creating a new reference to the same allocation as an existing reference counted pointer",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "is done using the ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "Clone",
                    "style": "InlineCode"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " trait implemented for ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "Rc<T>",
                    "style": "InlineCode",
                    "url": "http://docs.rust-lang.org/nightly/alloc/rc/struct.Rc.html"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " and ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "Weak<T>",
                    "style": "InlineCode",
                    "url": "http://docs.rust-lang.org/nightly/alloc/rc/struct.Weak.html"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": ".",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "codeBlock",
                "lang": "rust",
                "code": "use std::rc::Rc;\n\nlet foo = Rc::new(vec![1.0, 2.0, 3.0]);\n// The two syntaxes below are equivalent.\nlet a = foo.clone();\nlet b = Rc::clone(&foo);\n// a and b both point to the same memory location as foo."
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "spans": [
                  {
                    "text": "The ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "Rc::clone(&from)",
                    "style": "InlineCode"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " syntax is the most idiomatic because it conveys more explicitly",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "the meaning of the code. In the example above, this syntax makes it easier to see that",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "this code is creating a new reference rather than copying the whole content of foo.",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "heading",
                "level": "Title",
                "spans": [
                  {
                    "text": "Examples",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "spans": [
                  {
                    "text": "Consider a scenario where a set of ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "Gadget",
                    "style": "InlineCode"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "s are owned by a given ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "Owner",
                    "style": "InlineCode"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": ".",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "We want to have our ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "Gadget",
                    "style": "InlineCode"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "s point to their ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "Owner",
                    "style": "InlineCode"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": ". We can't do this with",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "unique ownership, because more than one gadget may belong to the same",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "Owner",
                    "style": "InlineCode"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": ". ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "Rc",
                    "style": "InlineCode",
                    "url": "http://docs.rust-lang.org/nightly/alloc/rc/struct.Rc.html"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " allows us to share an ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "Owner",
                    "style": "InlineCode"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " between multiple ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "Gadget",
                    "style": "InlineCode"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "s,",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "and have the ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "Owner",
                    "style": "InlineCode"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " remain allocated as long as any ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "Gadget",
                    "style": "InlineCode"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " points at it.",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "codeBlock",
                "lang": "rust",
                "code": "use std::rc::Rc;\n\nstruct Owner {\n    name: String,\n    // ...other fields\n}\n\nstruct Gadget {\n    id: i32,\n    owner: Rc<Owner>,\n    // ...other fields\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    // Create a reference-counted `Owner`.\n    let gadget_owner: Rc<Owner> = Rc::new(\n        Owner {\n            name: \"Gadget Man\".to_string(),\n        }\n    );\n\n    // Create `Gadget`s belonging to `gadget_owner`. Cloning the `Rc<Owner>`\n    // gives us a new pointer to the same `Owner` allocation, incrementing\n    // the reference count in the process.\n    let gadget1 = Gadget {\n        id: 1,\n        owner: Rc::clone(&gadget_owner),\n    };\n    let gadget2 = Gadget {\n        id: 2,\n        owner: Rc::clone(&gadget_owner),\n    };\n\n    // Dispose of our local variable `gadget_owner`.\n    drop(gadget_owner);\n\n    // Despite dropping `gadget_owner`, we're still able to print out the name\n    // of the `Owner` of the `Gadget`s. This is because we've only dropped a\n    // single `Rc<Owner>`, not the `Owner` it points to. As long as there are\n    // other `Rc<Owner>` pointing at the same `Owner` allocation, it will remain\n    // live. The field projection `gadget1.owner.name` works because\n    // `Rc<Owner>` automatically dereferences to `Owner`.\n    println!(\"Gadget {} owned by {}\", gadget1.id, gadget1.owner.name);\n    println!(\"Gadget {} owned by {}\", gadget2.id, gadget2.owner.name);\n\n    // At the end of the function, `gadget1` and `gadget2` are destroyed, and\n    // with them the last counted references to our `Owner`. Gadget Man now\n    // gets destroyed as well.\n}"
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "spans": [
                  {
                    "text": "If our requirements change, and we also need to be able to traverse from",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "Owner",
                    "style": "InlineCode"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " to ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "Gadget",
                    "style": "InlineCode"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": ", we will run into problems. An ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "Rc",
                    "style": "InlineCode",
                    "url": "http://docs.rust-lang.org/nightly/alloc/rc/struct.Rc.html"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " pointer from ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "Owner",
                    "style": "InlineCode"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "to ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "Gadget",
                    "style": "InlineCode"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " introduces a cycle. This means that their",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "reference counts can never reach 0, and the allocation will never be destroyed:",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "a memory leak. In order to get around this, we can use ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "Weak",
                    "style": "InlineCode",
                    "url": "http://docs.rust-lang.org/nightly/alloc/rc/struct.Weak.html"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "pointers.",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "spans": [
                  {
                    "text": "Rust actually makes it somewhat difficult to produce this loop in the first",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "place. In order to end up with two values that point at each other, one of",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "them needs to be mutable. This is difficult because ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "Rc",
                    "style": "InlineCode",
                    "url": "http://docs.rust-lang.org/nightly/alloc/rc/struct.Rc.html"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " enforces",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "memory safety by only giving out shared references to the value it wraps,",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "and these don't allow direct mutation. We need to wrap the part of the",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "value we wish to mutate in a ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "RefCell",
                    "style": "InlineCode",
                    "url": "https://doc.rust-lang.org/nightly/core/cell/index.html?search=core%3A%3Acell%3A%3ARefCell"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": ", which provides ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "interior",
                    "style": "Emphasis"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "mutability",
                    "style": "Emphasis"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": ": a method to achieve mutability through a shared reference.",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "RefCell",
                    "style": "InlineCode",
                    "url": "https://doc.rust-lang.org/nightly/core/cell/index.html?search=core%3A%3Acell%3A%3ARefCell"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " enforces Rust's borrowing rules at runtime.",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "codeBlock",
                "lang": "rust",
                "code": "use std::rc::Rc;\nuse std::rc::Weak;\nuse std::cell::RefCell;\n\nstruct Owner {\n    name: String,\n    gadgets: RefCell<Vec<Weak<Gadget>>>,\n    // ...other fields\n}\n\nstruct Gadget {\n    id: i32,\n    owner: Rc<Owner>,\n    // ...other fields\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    // Create a reference-counted `Owner`. Note that we've put the `Owner`'s\n    // vector of `Gadget`s inside a `RefCell` so that we can mutate it through\n    // a shared reference.\n    let gadget_owner: Rc<Owner> = Rc::new(\n        Owner {\n            name: \"Gadget Man\".to_string(),\n            gadgets: RefCell::new(vec![]),\n        }\n    );\n\n    // Create `Gadget`s belonging to `gadget_owner`, as before.\n    let gadget1 = Rc::new(\n        Gadget {\n            id: 1,\n            owner: Rc::clone(&gadget_owner),\n        }\n    );\n    let gadget2 = Rc::new(\n        Gadget {\n            id: 2,\n            owner: Rc::clone(&gadget_owner),\n        }\n    );\n\n    // Add the `Gadget`s to their `Owner`.\n    {\n        let mut gadgets = gadget_owner.gadgets.borrow_mut();\n        gadgets.push(Rc::downgrade(&gadget1));\n        gadgets.push(Rc::downgrade(&gadget2));\n\n        // `RefCell` dynamic borrow ends here.\n    }\n\n    // Iterate over our `Gadget`s, printing their details out.\n    for gadget_weak in gadget_owner.gadgets.borrow().iter() {\n\n        // `gadget_weak` is a `Weak<Gadget>`. Since `Weak` pointers can't\n        // guarantee the allocation still exists, we need to call\n        // `upgrade`, which returns an `Option<Rc<Gadget>>`.\n        //\n        // In this case we know the allocation still exists, so we simply\n        // `unwrap` the `Option`. In a more complicated program, you might\n        // need graceful error handling for a `None` result.\n\n        let gadget = gadget_weak.upgrade().unwrap();\n        println!(\"Gadget {} owned by {}\", gadget.id, gadget.owner.name);\n    }\n\n    // At the end of the function, `gadget_owner`, `gadget1`, and `gadget2`\n    // are destroyed. There are now no strong (`Rc`) pointers to the\n    // gadgets, so they are destroyed. This zeroes the reference count on\n    // Gadget Man, so he gets destroyed as well.\n}"
              }
            ],
            "level": "SingleLine"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "path": "slice",
        "kind": "module",
        "url": "http://docs.rust-lang.org/nightly/alloc/slice/index.html",
        "docs": [
          {
            "type": "truncatedBlock",
            "nodes": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "spans": [
                  {
                    "text": "Utilities for the slice primitive type.",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "spans": [
                  {
                    "text": "See also the slice primitive type",
                    "style": "Emphasis",
                    "url": "https://doc.rust-lang.org/nightly/core/index.html?search=core%3A%3Aslice"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": ".",
                    "style": "Emphasis"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
